# aeneid-019 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
TITLE = "Harpyiae. Buthrotum"
TITLE_EN = "The Harpies and the Voyage to Buthrotum — Aeneid III.209–305"
PART = "The Harpies and the Voyage to Buthrotum (III.209–305)"
ABOUT = "Aeneas, still telling Dido the story of his wanderings, has just left Crete behind, and the storm has driven the Trojans to the Strophades, islands in the Ionian Sea where the Harpies live, the bird-women who once plagued king Phineus. The Trojans find unguarded herds, kill and cook some of the cattle and call on the gods, even Jupiter, to share the meal, but the Harpies swoop down and foul the food; when they come a second time the Trojans are waiting with swords, though the birds' feathers turn every blow aside. Celaeno, one of the Harpies and a prophetess, perches on a high rock and accuses them of carrying war to the innocent; she announces on Apollo's authority that they will reach Italy but will not wall their city before hunger drives them to gnaw their very tables. The Trojans lose heart, and Anchises prays to the gods to avert the threat and spare the pious. They sail on past the islands of Odysseus's kingdom, Zacynthos, Dulichium, Same, Neritos and Ithaca, whose land they curse as the nurse of cruel Ulysses, and put in at Actium under the headland of Leucata, where they hold games and Aeneas hangs a captured Greek shield on a temple door with a verse of dedication. After a winter they sail on, past Corfu and along the coast of Epirus to Buthrotum, where they hear an unbelievable rumour: Helenus, a son of Priam, is king in a Greek city and has married Andromache. Aeneas goes ashore, burning to speak with him, and comes upon Andromache herself in a grove, pouring offerings to the ashes of Hector at an empty tomb."
U = [

((210, 'accipiunt;'),
"Saved from the waves, the shores of the Strophades\nfirst receive me;",
"Rescued from the waves, I am first taken in by the shores of the Strophades;",
"The part opens on a new paragraph, with Aeneas still the narrator. 'Servatum… me' is the perfect passive participle, 'saved', agreeing with 'me', the object of 'accipiunt': 'the shores receive me, saved from the waves'. 'ex undis' is 'out of the waves', and 'primum' is 'first', before any other landing. 'Strophadum' is a Greek genitive plural of the Strophades; the next clause tells what they are. 'Servatum' is singular because Aeneas, though he speaks for the whole fleet, puts himself at the centre (a common epic way of telling a voyage). The Latin Library reads 'excipiunt' for Greenough's 'accipiunt', a difference of nuance only."),

((213, 'priores.'),
"the Strophades, stand named with a Greek name,\nislands in the great Ionian sea, which dread Celaeno\nand the other Harpies inhabit, since Phineus's\nhouse was shut to them, and they left their earlier tables.",
"They are islands in the broad Ionian sea, and the Greeks gave them their name; terrible Celaeno and the other Harpies live there, ever since Phineus's house was closed against them and they quit the tables they had before.",
"'Strophades… dictae' — 'called the Strophades' (the name is Greek, from a word for 'turning', and 'Graio nomine' is an ablative of description, 'with a Greek name'). 'stant' here is 'stand', 'are'. 'Ionio in magno' is the preposition set between the adjectives (you'll see this constantly), for 'in magno Ionio'. 'quas… colunt' — 'which… inhabit', with two subjects, 'dira Celaeno' and 'Harpyiae aliae'. The Harpies are winged women-birds, spirits of storm-snatching; in the story told of the Argonauts, they tormented the blind Thracian king Phineus by fouling or snatching his food until the sons of Boreas drove them off, which is the allusion in 'Phineia… domus'. 'postquam clausa (est) domus' — 'since the house was closed', with 'est' understood, 'clausa' being the perfect passive participle. 'liquere' is the old short perfect form for 'liquerunt' (-ere = -erunt, you'll see this constantly), 'they left', and 'priores' is 'former, previous'."),

((215, 'undis.'),
"No monster is more grim than they, nor has any\nplague or wrath of the gods more savage lifted itself from Stygian waves.",
"No monster is more dismal than these, and no fiercer scourge, sent by the anger of heaven, ever rose from the waters of the Styx.",
"'Tristius haud illis monstrum' — 'no monster is grimmer than they' ('tristius' is a comparative; 'haud' negatives it; 'illis' is an ablative of comparison, 'than they', with no 'quam'). 'nec saevior ulla pestis' — 'nor any more savage plague'; 'pestis' is feminine, hence 'ulla'. 'ira deum' — 'the wrath of gods'; 'deum' is the old genitive plural (-um for -orum, you'll see this constantly). 'sese extulit' — 'raised itself out', 'sese' being the emphatic form of 'se'. The Styx is the river of the underworld, so 'Stygiis undis' sets their origin in the world below. The sentence is not finished: the next line gives their description."),

((218, 'fame.'),
"Maidens' faces the birds' have, the filthiest discharge\nof the belly, and hooked hands, and faces ever pale\nwith hunger.",
"They have the faces of maidens upon the bodies of birds; a most foul flux comes from their bellies; their hands are hooked, and their faces are always wan with hunger.",
"The sentence begun at 214 is completed here, as a list of nouns with the verb 'have' or 'are' understood. 'Virginei volucrum voltus' — 'the faces of maidens, the faces of the birds' ('volucrum' is a genitive plural of the noun 'volucris', 'bird'; 'voltus' is Greenough's spelling of 'vultus'). 'foedissima… proluvies' — 'the foulest outflow', 'proluvies' being a nominative singular in -es; 'ventris' is 'of the belly'. 'uncae manus' — 'hooked hands', that is, claws. 'pallida… ora' — 'pale faces' (note 'ora' is the plural of 'os, oris', 'face', 'mouth'), and 'fame' is an ablative of cause, 'with hunger'. The Latin Library prints 'uultus', the same word."),

((221, 'herbas.'),
"Hither when borne we entered the harbours, behold,\nglad herds of cattle we see all over the fields,\nand a flock of goat-born ones with no keeper along the grass.",
"As soon as we had been carried here and sailed into the harbours, we saw, to our delight, herds of cattle scattered over the fields and a flock of goats with no one to watch them on the grass.",
"A new paragraph. 'Huc ubi delati portus intravimus' — 'when carried here we entered the harbours'; 'delati' is the perfect passive participle of 'defero', nominative plural agreeing with the 'we' of 'intravimus'. 'portus' here is accusative plural. 'ecce' is 'behold'; it is a vivid pointing word. 'laeta… armenta' — 'glad herds', but 'laetus' also means 'thriving, fat', so the herds are both fine and a sign of luck. 'caprigenum pecus' — 'the flock of goat-born': 'caprigenum' is the genitive plural of 'caprigenus' used instead of the usual 'caprigenorum' (-um for -orum again), and it stands for 'goats'. 'nullo custode' is a phrase in the ablative, 'no keeper', in the style of an ablative absolute where a participle is not needed."),

((223, 'Iovem;'),
"We rush on them with the sword, and the gods and Jove himself we call\nto a share and to the plunder;",
"We fell on them with our swords and called on the gods, and on Jupiter himself, to come and share in the spoil;",
"'Inruimus ferro' — 'we rush on (them) with the iron', 'ferro' being the ablative of means, 'by sword'; it is the plain poetic word for the blade. 'divos' is Greenough's accusative plural (-os for the usual 'divos'/'deos'). 'in partem praedamque' — 'to a share and to the booty', a loose pair: they invite the gods in to share what they take. 'ipsumque… Iovem' puts Jupiter, the king of gods, among those invited. The sentence continues in the next unit."),

((224, 'opimis.'),
"then on the curved shore\nwe both build couches and feast on rich fare.",
"and then on the curving shore we set out couches and feasted on a bountiful meal.",
"'tum litore curvo' — 'then on the curved shore'; 'litore' is the ablative of place with no preposition (you'll see this constantly in poetry). 'exstruimusque toros' — 'we build up couches' (the 'toros' are the heaped couches or seats of turf for a banquet), with '-que… -que' set across the two verbs, 'and… and', joining two actions: 'tum… exstruimusque… epulamur'. 'epulamur' is a deponent verb (passive in form, active in sense): 'we feast'. 'dapibusque… opimis' — 'with rich feasts', the ablative of means."),

((228, 'immundo;'),
"But suddenly with a horrifying swoop from the mountains the Harpies\nare upon us, and with great clangs they beat their wings,\nand they snatch the feasts, and everything they befoul\nwith unclean touch;",
"But suddenly, with a terrible swoop from the hills, the Harpies were on us, beating their wings with a great clatter; they tore away the food and fouled it all with their filthy touch;",
"'At subitae… adsunt Harpyiae' — 'suddenly the Harpies are present'; 'subitae' is an adjective with the force of an adverb, a Latin habit ('subitae' agrees with 'Harpyiae'). 'horrifico lapsu' — 'with a terrifying swoop', ablative of manner. 'magnis… clangoribus' — 'with great clangings', ablative of means, with 'quatiunt alas', 'they shake their wings'. 'diripiuntque dapes' — 'and they tear the feasts to pieces', that is, snatch. 'contactuque… immundo' — 'by an unclean touch', ablative of means, 'contactus' being a fourth-declension noun; 'omnia foedant' — 'defile everything'. The narration moves between past and present (the 'historic present', 'adsunt', 'quatiunt'), as at 219–224: a Latin way of making a scene vivid."),

((228, 'odorem.'),
"then a dread cry amid a foul stench.",
"and with the stench came a dreadful cry.",
"A verbless clause, 'tum (est) vox dira inter taetrum odorem' — 'then (there is) a dread voice amid a foul smell'. 'inter' follows 'dira' rather than standing before its noun (anastrophe, a word shifted out of its usual place), and 'taetrum' is the adjective of 'odorem' put in front of the whole phrase. The word 'dira' is the key word of the passage: it is used again of 'dira fames' (256), the Harpies' own threat."),

((231, 'ignem:'),
"Again in a deep retreat, beneath a hollowed rock,\nshut in all around by trees and by bristling shades,\nwe set out the tables and put back fire on the altars:",
"Next we go to a deep recess under a hollowed rock, closed in on every side by trees and by shadows that bristle with branches, and set out the tables once more and lay a fire again on the altars.",
"'Rursum in secessu longo' — 'again in a long retreat'; the 'secessus' is a secluded recess (a cove or hollow) away from the herds. 'sub rupe cavata' — 'under a hollowed rock'. 'arboribus clausi circum atque horrentibus umbris' — 'closed all round by trees and bristling shadows'; 'clausi' is the perfect passive participle, nominative plural, with ablative of means; 'circum' here is an adverb, 'round about'. 'horrentibus' means 'bristling, shaggy', and the shadows bristle with branches. 'arisque reponimus ignem' — 'and we put the fire back on the altars' (the prefix 're-' means 'again' or 'back': they redo the sacrifice); 'aris' is the dative with the compound verb. The colon introduces the second attack."),

((234, 'dapes.'),
"again from the opposite quarter of the sky and from blind hiding-places\na noisy crowd flies round the plunder on hooked feet,\nand pollutes the feast with its mouth.",
"Again, from a different quarter of the sky, out of hidden lairs, the clamouring flock wheels about the spoil on its hooked claws and fouls the food with its mouths.",
"'rursum ex diverso caeli' — 'again from the opposite part of the sky' ('ex diverso' means 'from the opposite direction'; 'caeli' is the genitive, 'of the sky'). 'caecisque latebris' — 'and blind hiding places', where 'caecus' means 'unseen, hidden', not 'blind'. 'turba sonans' — 'the crowd, sounding, noisy'. 'praedam pedibus circumvolat uncis' — 'flies round the plunder with hooked feet' (ablative of means; 'circumvolat' takes an accusative). 'ore' is the ablative of means, 'with the mouth', the singular put for the plural of all their mouths. The sentence ends partway through the line, at the stop after 'dapes'."),

((235, 'gerendum.'),
"To my comrades then I proclaim: let them take up arms,\nand that war must be waged with the dread race.",
"Then I give my men an order: they are to take up their weapons, for we must make war on this horrible tribe.",
"'Sociis… edico' — 'to my comrades I proclaim, I order' ('edico' with a dative). 'arma capessant' is a command in the subjunctive without 'ut' (an indirect command, 'that they take up arms'; 'capesso' is a frequentative of 'capio'). 'et dira bellum cum gente gerendum' — 'and that war must be waged with the dread race', completing the order with 'esse' understood: 'bellum gerendum (esse)', an accusative and infinitive with the gerundive of obligation (you'll see this constantly). The word order puts 'bellum' after 'gente', in the middle of the phrase it governs."),

((237, 'condunt.'),
"No otherwise than ordered they do, and the swords\nthey set hidden here and there through the grass, and they conceal the shields that lie unseen.",
"They do exactly as they are told: they place their swords here and there, hidden in the grass, and they stow away their shields out of sight.",
"'Haud secus ac iussi faciunt' — 'not otherwise than ordered they do', 'iussi' being the perfect passive participle of 'iubeo', 'having been ordered'; 'haud secus ac' means 'just as, no differently from'. 'tectos… enses' — 'hidden swords' ('tectos' in agreement with 'enses'), 'per herbam', 'through the grass'. 'disponunt' means 'they arrange in separate places'. 'scuta latentia condunt' — 'they hide the shields that lie hidden', the participle 'latentia' (neuter plural of 'latens') being a deliberate pairing with 'condunt'. The Latin Library has 'ensis', the accusative plural spelled with -is; the sense is the same."),

((240, 'cavo.'),
"Then when, swooping down, they gave their noise along the curved\nshores, from a high lookout Misenus gives the signal\nwith hollow bronze.",
"Then, when the Harpies swept down and made their din along the winding shore, Misenus gave the signal from a high watch-post with his hollow bronze.",
"'Ergo ubi delapsae sonitum per curva dedere litora' — 'so when, having glided down, they gave their sound along the curving shores'; 'delapsae' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'delabor', feminine plural (the Harpies); 'dedere' is the old perfect 'dederunt' (-ere = -erunt, you'll see this constantly). 'dat signum… Misenus' — Misenus is the Trojan trumpeter, who will die and be buried at Cumae in Book 6. 'specula… ab alta' — 'from a high lookout'; 'specula' is a watch-post. 'aere cavo' — 'with hollow bronze', ablative of means, 'aes' being the common poetic word for a bronze trumpet. Greenough's sentence stops after 'cavo.' in the middle of line 240."),

((241, 'volucres:'),
"Our comrades charge, and try a strange sort of battle,\nto defile the filthy birds of the sea with the sword:",
"Our men rush in and try an unheard-of kind of fight, to wound the foul birds of the sea with steel;",
"'Invadunt socii' — 'the comrades attack'. 'nova proelia temptant' — 'they try new battles', 'new' in the sense of strange, since the enemy is a bird. 'obscenas pelagi… volucres' — 'the foul birds of the sea'; 'obscenas' means 'foul, ill-omened' and 'pelagi' is the genitive of 'pelagus', 'the sea', a Greek word (neuter, in -us). 'foedare' is the infinitive 'to defile, mutilate', a poetic infinitive explaining 'proelia temptant', 'they try the battle to…'. The colon introduces the result in the next line."),

((244, 'relinquunt.'),
"but neither force on their feathers nor wounds on their backs\nthey take, and in swift flight, up under the stars, gliding,\nthey leave their half-eaten prey and foul traces.",
"But they take no blow on their plumage and no wound in their backs; in swift flight they glide up beneath the stars and leave behind their half-eaten spoil and their foul traces.",
"'sed neque vim plumis ullam nec volnera tergo accipiunt' — 'but they receive neither any force on their feathers nor wounds on their back'; 'volnera' is Greenough's spelling of 'vulnera'. The Harpies are supernatural, and weapons cannot hurt them. 'celerique fuga sub sidera lapsae' — 'gliding in swift flight under (up to) the stars'; 'lapsae' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'labor', feminine plural, and 'sub' with the accusative means 'up to, towards'. 'semesam praedam' — 'the half-eaten prey' ('semesus' is the participle of 'semiedo'), and 'vestigia foeda' are the foul traces, that is, their droppings."),

((246, 'vocem:'),
"One on a very high rock sat down, Celaeno,\nill-omened prophetess, and breaks this speech from her breast:",
"One of them perched on a towering crag, Celaeno, a prophetess of ill luck, and out of her breast she burst this utterance:",
"A new paragraph. 'Una… Celaeno' — 'one, Celaeno'; inthe first word picks out a single Harpy, and the name is placed last. 'praecelsa' means 'very high'. 'consedit' is the perfect of 'consido', 'sat, perched'. 'infelix vates' — 'ill-fated, ill-omened prophetess'; 'vates' is the usual word for an inspired seer and is here feminine. 'rumpit… vocem' — 'she breaks (forth) a voice', a poetic phrase for starting to speak. The colon opens her speech, which Greenough prints with a mark of quotation."),

((249, 'regno?'),
"“War even for the slaughter of oxen and bullocks laid low,\nsons of Laomedon, war too do you make ready to bring,\nand to drive the guiltless Harpies from their father's kingdom?",
"“Is it war, then, in return for slain oxen and bullocks struck down, descendants of Laomedon, is it war that you are preparing to bring, and to drive the innocent Harpies out of their ancestral realm?",
"Celaeno's speech opens (the mark “ is Greenough's). 'Bellum etiam' — 'war too', 'etiam' marking it as an extra insult on top of the slaughter. 'pro caede boum stratisque iuvencis' — 'in return for the killing of oxen and the bullocks laid low'; 'pro' with the ablative, here 'for, in exchange for'. 'Laomedontiadae' is a vocative, 'sons of Laomedon': Laomedon, the king of Troy, cheated gods of their pay and broke his word, and the title taunts the Trojans with the faith-breaking of their ancestor. 'bellumne inferre paratis' — 'do you prepare to bring war?', with '-ne' marking a question, repeating 'bellum' for emphasis. 'patrio… regno' — ablative of separation, 'from the ancestral kingdom'. 'insontis' is an accusative plural in -is. 'Harpyias' is the Greek accusative plural."),

((252, 'pando.'),
"Take it, then, in your minds, and fix these words of mine,\nwhich the almighty Father to Phoebus, Phoebus Apollo to me\nforetold; to you I, greatest of the Furies, lay them open.",
"So take it to heart, then, and fasten these words of mine in your memory: the Father who rules all told them first to Phoebus, and Phoebus Apollo told them to me, and I, the greatest of the Furies, now set them out for you.",
"'Accipite ergo animis atque haec mea figite dicta' — two imperatives, 'accept' and 'fix'; 'animis' is the ablative of place, 'in your minds', and 'dicta' is 'sayings, words'. 'quae Phoebo pater omnipotens, mihi Phoebus Apollo praedixit' — the relative 'quae' is the object of 'praedixit', 'foretold', and a single verb serves two subjects, 'pater omnipotens' (Jupiter) and 'Phoebus Apollo', with two datives, 'Phoebo' and 'mihi': the prophecy passes from Jupiter to Apollo to Celaeno. 'Furiarum ego maxuma' — 'I, the greatest of the Furies' ('Furiarum' is a partitive genitive; 'maxuma' is Greenough's spelling); Celaeno claims the rank of a Fury here, avengers of crime. 'pando' means 'I lay open, reveal'."),

((254, 'licebit;'),
"For Italy you aim by your course, and with the winds called\nyou will go to Italy, and to enter its harbours will be allowed;",
"You are bound for Italy, and with the winds you have called on you shall go to Italy and be allowed to enter its harbours;",
"'Italiam cursu petitis' — 'you seek Italy by your course', the present tense with the force of 'you are on your way to'. 'ventisque vocatis' is an ablative absolute, 'the winds having been called', that is, invoked by prayer, so favourable. 'ibitis Italiam' — 'you will go to Italy', the future of 'eo'; the accusative of the goal is used without a preposition with a country's name, as with a town (a poetic use). 'portusque intrare licebit' — 'and it will be permitted (to you) to enter harbours', 'licebit' being the impersonal verb with an infinitive as its subject. The semicolon prepares the contrast with 'sed'."),

((257, 'mensas.”'),
"but not before you gird the granted city with walls,\nthan dread hunger and the wrong of our slaughter\ndrive you to eat up the tables gnawed round with your jaws.”",
"but you shall not ring with walls the city granted to you until terrible hunger, and the wrong done in killing our cattle, force you in your jaws to gnaw your very tables and eat them away.”",
"'sed non ante datam cingetis moenibus urbem quam…' — 'but not before you gird the given city with walls than…', 'ante… quam' set apart, 'quam' only coming in the next line. 'datam urbem' — the city granted by fate. The verb after 'quam' is the subjunctive 'subigat', which expresses something awaited or intended, 'before hunger shall drive you'. 'dira fames' — 'dread hunger'. 'nostraeque iniuria caedis' — 'and the wrong of our slaughter', the 'nostrae' being the Harpies' own, referring to the killing of their cattle. 'subigat… absumere' — 'drive (you) to eat up'. 'ambesas… mensas' — 'tables eaten round'; 'malis' is the ablative of means, 'with jaws' ('mala' is the jaw). This curse comes true in Book 7, where the Trojans eat the flat loaves they have set their food on. The closing mark ” belongs to the end of the speech."),

((258, 'refugit.'),
"She spoke, and, borne off on her wings, fled back into the forest.",
"So she spoke, and on her wings she was carried off and fled back into the wood.",
"'Dixit' is the tag that ends a speech (you'll see this constantly). 'pennis ablata' — 'carried off on her wings', 'ablata' being the perfect passive participle of 'aufero', feminine, with 'pennis' in the ablative of means. 'refugit' — 'fled back' (the prefix 're-' marks the return to where she came from)."),

((260, 'deriguit;'),
"But to my comrades with sudden fear the cold blood\nstiffened;",
"But a sudden terror froze the blood of my comrades, chilling it where it ran;",
"A new paragraph. 'At sociis… gelidus… sanguis deriguit' — 'but to my comrades the cold blood stiffened'; 'sociis' is a dative of the person affected, 'for/to the comrades', and 'subita… formidine' is an ablative of cause, 'with sudden fear'. 'deriguit' is the perfect of 'derigesco', 'it stiffened'."),

((262, 'volucres.'),
"their spirits fell, and no longer with arms,\nbut with vows and prayers they bid me ask for peace,\nwhether they be goddesses or dread and foul birds.",
"their courage failed, and they urge me to seek peace no longer by weapons but by promises and prayers, whether these creatures be goddesses or only terrible and filthy birds.",
"'cecidere animi' — 'the spirits fell'; 'cecidere' is the old form of 'ceciderunt' (-ere = -erunt, you'll see this constantly). 'nec iam amplius armis, sed votis precibusque' — 'no longer with arms, but with vows and prayers' (ablatives of means). 'iubent exposcere pacem' — 'they bid (us) demand peace'; the object of 'iubent' is left unexpressed, 'socii' being the subject, and 'exposcere' is the infinitive 'to ask earnestly'. 'sive deae, seu sint dirae obscenaeque volucres' — 'whether (they are) goddesses or are dread and foul birds'; 'deae' is the nominative plural, 'sint' is a subjunctive of an alternative question reported indirectly, and 'sive… seu' is simply 'whether… or'. The sailors do not know what the Harpies are."),

((264, 'honores:'),
"And father Anchises, with palms spread out from the shore,\ncalls the great powers, and proclaims the honours deserved:",
"And father Anchises, holding out his palms from the shore, called on the great divinities and appointed the offerings due to them:",
"'passis de litore palmis' — 'with palms spread out, from the shore' ('passis' is the perfect participle of 'pando', 'spread', not of 'patior'; 'palmis' means 'palms of the hands', the usual gesture of prayer to heaven). 'numina magna' — 'the great powers'; a 'numen' is a divine will or presence. 'meritosque indicit honores' — 'and proclaims the due honours', the rites and offerings the gods have earned; 'meritos' is the participle of the deponent 'mereor' with passive sense, 'deserved'. The colon introduces his prayer, whose opening mark “ stands at 265."),

((266, 'pios!'),
"“Gods, ward off these threats; gods, turn aside such a mishap,\nand kindly save the pious!”",
"“Gods, keep these threats away; gods, avert so dire a mischance, and in your kindness spare those who do their duty!”",
"The prayer opens with the mark “. 'Di' is the contracted plural of 'deus' (here a vocative, 'O gods'), repeated for solemnity. 'prohibete minas' — 'keep off the threats'; 'talem avertite casum' — 'turn away such a mishap'. 'placidi servate pios' — 'kindly (placidi, nominative plural agreeing with the gods) save the pious'; 'pios' is the accusative plural used as a noun, 'those who are dutiful', a word the poem uses of Aeneas himself and of his people. In Greenough's text the closing mark is printed after a space and with no space before the next word ('pios! ”Tum'), so a cut after the mark would fall in the middle of a word: this unit stops at 'pios!', the closing mark is carried in `l` and `i` here, and in the edition it stands at the head of the next unit's `t`."),

((267, 'rudentes.'),
"”Then from the shore to tear off the cable, and to loosen\nthe ropes shaken out, he bids.",
"Then he orders us to cast off the cable from the shore and shake out and slacken the ropes.",
"The edition prints the closing mark of Anchises's prayer glued to 'Tum', so it stands at the head of this unit's `t`. 'Tum… iubet' — 'then he orders'; the subject is Anchises, the object (us) is understood, and the two infinitives 'deripere' and 'laxare' depend on 'iubet'. 'litore funem deripere' — 'to tear the cable from the shore', 'litore' being the ablative of separation, 'funem' the mooring cable. 'excussosque… rudentes' — 'and the ropes shaken out'; 'excussos' is the perfect passive participle, 'rudentes' the ropes of the rigging (accusative plural). The Latin Library prints 'rudentis' for the same case."),

((268, 'Noti;'),
"The south winds stretch the sails;",
"The south winds fill and stretch the sails;",
"'Tendunt vela Noti' — 'the Souths stretch the sails'. 'Noti' is the nominative plural of 'Notus', the south wind, and here the winds as a force. 'tendunt' means 'stretch taut'. A short unit, cut at the semicolon."),

((269, 'vocabat.'),
"we flee over foaming waves,\nwhere both wind and helmsman were calling our course.",
"and we flee over the foaming waters, wherever the wind and the helmsman called our course.",
"'fugimus spumantibus undis' — 'we flee on the foaming waves' (ablative of the route). 'qua' is 'where, by which way'. 'cursum ventusque gubernatorque vocabat' — 'both wind and helmsman were calling (the) course'; the singular verb 'vocabat' agrees with the nearer of two subjects, and 'vocare cursum' is 'to summon a course', to point the way. The double '-que… -que' ties the two subjects together."),

((271, 'saxis.'),
"Now in the middle of the swell wooded Zacynthos appears,\nand Dulichium, and Same, and Neritos steep with crags.",
"Soon, in the midst of the swell, wooded Zacynthos comes into view, with Dulichium, Same and Neritos, towering with its rocks.",
"'Iam medio adparet fluctu' — 'now in the middle of the swell appears' ('adparet' is Greenough's spelling of 'apparet', and 'medio fluctu' an ablative of place). Zacynthos, Dulichium, Same and Neritos are islands off the west coast of Greece in the kingdom of Ulysses, as Homer names them in the Odyssey. 'nemorosa' means 'wooded'. 'Dulichiumque Sameque et Neritos' — the '-que… et' joins the three names; 'Same' is a Greek first-declension nominative. 'ardua saxis' — 'steep with rocks', an ablative of respect, 'saxis' being the rocks. The scanner flags this line; it needs a hand scansion."),

((273, 'Ulixi.'),
"We escape the rocks of Ithaca, the kingdom of Laertes,\nand we curse the land that nursed fierce Ulysses.",
"We slip past the crags of Ithaca, the realm of Laertes, and we curse the land that bred the cruel Ulysses.",
"'Effugimus scopulos Ithacae' — 'we escape the rocks of Ithaca'. 'Laertia regna' is in apposition to Ithaca: 'the Laertian realm', the kingdom of Laertes, who was the father of Ulysses. 'terram altricem' — 'the nurse land' ('altricem' is the accusative of 'altrix', a feminine noun used as an adjective, 'nourishing'), the land that bred him. 'saevi exsecramur Ulixi' — 'we curse… of savage Ulixes'; 'Ulixi' is taken here as a genitive (it is a Greek name that Latin sometimes declines on the model of the second declension), 'saevi' agreeing with it. Ulysses is hated as the Greek whose tricks destroyed Troy, which is why the Trojans curse his homeland."),

((275, 'Apollo.'),
"Soon the cloudy peaks of Mount Leucata too\nand Apollo, feared by sailors, is opened to view.",
"Next the cloud-covered summits of the Leucatan hill, and Apollo, whom sailors dread, are laid open to sight.",
"'Mox et Leucatae nimbosa cacumina montis' — 'soon, too, the cloudy summits of mount Leucata'; 'Leucatae' is the genitive singular of the Greek name Leucate (a headland on the island of Leucas, by Actium), agreeing with 'montis'. 'nimbosa' means 'full of clouds'. 'formidatus nautis… Apollo' — 'Apollo, dreaded by sailors', where 'nautis' is a dative of the agent with the perfect passive participle 'formidatus' (you'll see this constantly with perfect passives). 'aperitur' is 'is opened up, comes into view'. 'Apollo' means his temple on the headland."),

((276, 'urbi;'),
"This we seek, weary, and approach a small town;",
"This headland we make for, worn out, and we come up to a little town;",
"'Hunc' — the mount or the god's headland. 'petimus fessi' — 'we seek it, tired' (the adjective 'fessi' in the nominative plural agrees with 'we', for 'tired as we are'). 'parvae succedimus urbi' — 'we approach the small town'; 'succedo' takes a dative (here 'urbi') when it means 'go up to'."),

((277, 'puppes.'),
"the anchor is thrown from the prow, the sterns stand on the shore.",
"The anchor is cast from the bows, and the ships stand with their sterns on the beach.",
"'ancora de prora iacitur' — 'the anchor is thrown from the prow'; the verb is in the present passive, in the narrative present. 'stant litore puppes' — 'the sterns stand on the shore': 'puppes' (literally 'sterns') stands for 'ships', and 'litore' is again an ablative of place without a preposition."),

((280, 'ludis.'),
"So, having possessed at last the land unhoped for,\nwe purify ourselves to Jove and kindle the altars with offerings,\nand we throng the Actian shores with Trojan games.",
"So, having reached at last a land we had not hoped for, we hold the rite of purification to Jupiter, light the altars with our votive gifts and fill the shores of Actium with games in the Trojan manner.",
"A new paragraph. 'insperata tandem tellure potiti' — 'having got possession at last of the unhoped-for land'; 'potiti' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'potior', which takes the ablative ('tellure'). 'lustramurque Iovi' — 'we are purified (we purify ourselves) to Jove', a passive used in a middle sense; 'lustro' means to purify with a ritual, and 'Iovi' is the dative of the god honoured. 'votisque incendimus aras' — 'and with vows we kindle the altars', 'votis' being the offerings promised in prayer. 'Actiaque… litora' — 'the Actian shores'; Actium was to be the site of Octavian's victory in 31 BC, and Virgil's readers would have known it, so the games Aeneas holds there are the poem's early nod to the Augustan Actian games. 'Iliacis… ludis' — 'with Iliac (Trojan) games', ablative of means."),

((282, 'socii;'),
"My comrades, stripped, practise the wrestling of their fathers\nwith sliding oil;",
"My companions strip, and with the oil gliding over their limbs they practise the wrestling that their fathers knew;",
"'Exercent patrias oleo labente palaestras' — 'they exercise the ancestral wrestling-schools'; 'palaestras' stands for the exercise itself, and 'patrias' marks it as the Trojans' native sport. 'oleo labente' is an ablative absolute, 'the oil gliding', that is, the oil (rubbed on) running over their bodies. 'nudati socii' — 'the stripped comrades', 'nudati' being the perfect passive participle of 'nudo'. The sentence stops after 'socii;'."),

((283, 'hostis.'),
"it pleases to have got away from so many Argive cities\nand to have held our flight through the midst of enemies.",
"It is a joy to have escaped so many Greek cities, and to have kept our course of flight through the midst of enemies.",
"'iuvat evasisse tot urbes Argolicas' — 'it is pleasing to have escaped so many Argive cities'; 'iuvat' is an impersonal verb whose subject is the perfect infinitive 'evasisse', and 'evado' takes an accusative here. 'Argolicas' means 'of Argolis', that is, Greek, as 'Argive' does throughout the poem. 'mediosque fugam tenuisse per hostis' — 'and to have held a flight through the midst of enemies'; 'medios' agrees with 'hostis' (accusative plural in -is), and 'fugam tenuisse' is 'kept up a flight'."),

((285, 'undas.'),
"Meanwhile the sun rolls round the great year,\nand icy winter roughens the waves with north winds.",
"Meanwhile the sun completes its round of the great year, and icy winter rough with north winds whips up the waves.",
"'Interea magnum sol circumvolvitur annum' — 'meanwhile the sun rolls round the great year'; 'magnum annum' is an accusative of extent, 'through the great year', the whole circuit. 'circumvolvitur' is a passive with the sense 'rolls itself round'. 'glacialis hiemps' — 'icy winter' (Greenough's 'hiemps' is the old spelling of 'hiems'). 'aquilonibus asperat undas' — 'roughens the waves with north winds', 'aquilonibus' an ablative of means. The change of season tells us they have stayed at Actium through the winter."),

((287, 'signo:'),
"A shield of hollow bronze, the carried weight of great Abas,\nI fasten on the facing doorposts, and mark the deed with a verse:",
"A shield of hollow bronze, once the burden of mighty Abas, I fix to the doorposts that face me, and I mark the deed with a verse:",
"The print has a full stop where a comma is plainly meant: 'clipeum. magni gestamen Abantis' has a stop and then a lowercase 'magni', because 'magni gestamen Abantis' is in apposition to 'clipeum'. The reading translation corrects it to a comma;is kept exactly as Greenough prints it, and the unit keeps both lines together so the sentence is not broken at the misprinted stop. 'Aere cavo clipeum' — 'a shield of hollow bronze' ('aere cavo' is an ablative of material); 'gestamen' is 'what is carried'; 'magni Abantis' — 'of great Abas', a Greek warrior whose arms Aeneas took. 'postibus adversis figo' — 'I fasten on the opposite doorposts', that is, the door-posts of Apollo's temple; 'figo' is 'fix'. 'rem carmine signo' — 'I mark the thing with a verse', the dedication given next."),

((288, 'ARMA.'),
"AENEAS HAEC DE DANAIS VICTORIBVS ARMA.",
"Aeneas set up these arms, taken from the victorious Greeks.",
"The inscription, in capitals as Greenough prints it (VICTORIBVS with V for U, as on Roman stone). It is a complete hexameter with no verb: 'Aeneas (dedicates, set up) these arms from the victorious Danaans' — 'haec arma' is the object, 'de Danais victoribus' means 'from (taken from) the victorious Greeks', and a verb such as 'dat' or 'dicat' is understood, as is usual on dedications. 'Danais' is the ablative plural of 'Danai', 'the Greeks'. The scanner flags this line, so it needs a hand scansion."),

((289, 'transtris:'),
"To leave the harbours then I order, and to sit on the benches:",
"Then I order my men to quit the harbours and take their seats at the rowing-benches:",
"'Linquere tum portus iubeo et considere transtris' — 'to leave the harbours then I order, and to sit down on the benches'; the object of 'iubeo' (the comrades) is understood, and the two infinitives 'linquere' and 'considere' depend on it. 'portus' is an accusative plural. 'transtris' is the ablative of place with no preposition (the benches, 'transtra', are the rowers' seats across the ship)."),

((290, 'verrunt.'),
"in rivalry my comrades strike the sea and sweep the levels.",
"The crew strike the sea in rivalry, each trying to outdo the next, and sweep the flat water with their oars.",
"'certatim' is an adverb, 'in a contest, vying'. 'feriunt mare' — 'strike the sea' (with oars). 'aequora verrunt' — 'sweep the levels', 'aequora' being the plain surface of the sea, 'verrunt' being 'sweep, brush', a rowing word used of oars; the pairing is a simple two-part statement."),

((293, 'urbem.'),
"Straightway we hide the lofty citadels of the Phaeacians,\nand we skirt the coasts of Epirus and enter the\nChaonian harbour and approach the high city of Buthrotum.",
"Soon we have lost sight of the high towers of the Phaeacians behind us; we coast along the shores of Epirus, steer into the Chaonian harbour and come up to the lofty city of Buthrotum.",
"'Protinus aërias Phaeacum abscondimus arces' — 'straight on we hide the airy towers of the Phaeacians'; 'abscondimus' is a nautical way of saying that the land sinks out of sight behind the ship. 'aërias' (Greenough prints the diaeresis) means 'high in the air'; 'Phaeacum' is the genitive plural of 'Phaeaces' (-um for -orum again, you'll see this constantly): the Phaeacians are the Homeric sailors of Scheria (identified with Corfu), who took Ulysses home. 'litoraque Epiri legimus' — 'we skim the shores of Epirus' (a Latin sailor's phrase: 'lego' is 'gather up, pass close by'). 'portuque subimus Chaonio' — 'and we approach (enter) the Chaonian harbour', 'Chaonius' being an adjective for the district of Epirus. 'celsam Buthroti accedimus urbem' — 'and we draw near the high city of Buthrotum' ('Buthroti' is the genitive of the Greek name). The scanner flags 293: the 'Chaonio' needs a hand scansion."),

((297, 'marito.'),
"Here an unbelievable rumour of events seizes our ears,\nthat Helenus, Priam's son, rules over Greek cities,\nhaving won the marriage and the sceptres of Pyrrhus, grandson of Aeacus,\nand that Andromache has passed again to a husband of her own land.",
"Here an incredible report of happenings fills our ears: Priam's son Helenus is king in Greek cities, having won both the hand and the sceptre of Pyrrhus, descendant of Aeacus, and Andromache has passed a second time to a husband of her own people.",
"A new paragraph. 'incredibilis rerum fama' — 'an unbelievable rumour of things'; 'auris' is an accusative plural in -is. The rumour is given in an accusative and infinitive (the content of 'fama'): 'Priamiden Helenum… regnare' — 'that Helenus, Priam's son, rules' ('Priamiden' is a Greek patronymic, accusative). Helenus is a Trojan prophet-prince, a son of Priam. 'Graias… urbes' — 'Greek cities'. 'coniugio Aeacidae Pyrrhi sceptrisque potitum' — 'having got the marriage and sceptres of Pyrrhus the Aeacid'; 'potitum' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'potior', governing the ablatives, and 'Aeacidae' is the Greek genitive of 'Aeacides', a descendant of Aeacus (Pyrrhus is the son of Achilles, grandson of Peleus, great-grandson of Aeacus). 'et patrio Andromachen iterum cessisse marito' — 'and that Andromache had gone again to a husband of her own country'; 'Andromachen' is a Greek accusative, 'cessisse' a perfect infinitive, 'patrio… marito' a dative, 'a husband from her fatherland', and 'iterum' means that she has remarried: Pyrrhus carried her off from Troy, and now she belongs to another Trojan. The Latin Library has 'urbis' for Greenough's 'urbes', a case-ending variant that changes nothing."),

((299, 'tantos.'),
"I was struck dumb, and my heart was set on fire with wondrous\ndesire to address the man and learn of such great fortunes.",
"I stood amazed, and my heart burned with a strange longing to speak to the man and find out all that had befallen him.",
"'Obstipui' — 'I was stupefied'. 'miroque incensum pectus amore' — 'and my heart kindled with a wondrous longing', where 'incensum' is the perfect passive participle with 'est' understood, and 'miro amore' is an ablative of cause. The two infinitives 'compellare' ('to address') and 'cognoscere' ('to learn') explain the longing (you'll see this constantly: the infinitive after a noun of desire). 'virum' is the man, Helenus. 'casus… tantos' — 'such great fortunes, happenings'."),

((303, 'Andromache,'),
"I go forward from the harbour, leaving the fleet and the shore,\nwhen by chance the solemn feasts and the sad gifts\nbefore the city, in a grove, by the wave of a false Simois,\nwas pouring to the ashes Andromache,",
"I walked on from the harbour, leaving my fleet and the shore behind, and just then, by chance, before the city, in a grove, beside the water of a counterfeit Simois, Andromache was pouring out the yearly feast and the doleful gifts to the ashes of the dead,",
"'Progredior portu' — 'I go forward from the harbour' ('portu' is an ablative of separation). 'classis et litora linquens' — 'leaving the fleet and the shore', 'linquens' being the present participle. 'sollemnis cum forte dapes et tristia dona… libabat… Andromache' — a 'cum' clause with the indicative where the sense is 'when just then'; 'sollemnis' is the accusative plural in -is, 'solemn, yearly' (the funeral meal at fixed times), and 'libabat' is the imperfect of 'libo', 'was pouring out as an offering'. The word order puts the objects first and the subject last, keeping the reader waiting for the name. 'cineri' is a dative, 'to the ashes'. 'falsi Simoentis' — 'of a false Simois': Andromache has named a little stream after the river at Troy, a copy of her lost homeland; 'Simoentis' is the genitive of a Greek name."),

((305, 'aras.'),
"and she called the Shades to Hector's tomb, which empty\nwith green turf, and the twin altars, a cause for tears,\nshe had consecrated.",
"and she was calling the Shades to Hector's mound, an empty tomb of green turf which she had hallowed, with two altars besides, a cause of her tears.",
"'Manisque vocabat' — 'and she called the Manes' (the spirits of the dead; 'Manis' is an accusative plural in -is). 'Hectoreum ad tumulum' — 'to the tomb of Hector' ('Hectoreus' is the adjective), with 'ad' placed between adjective and noun. 'viridi quem caespite inanem et geminas… sacraverat aras' — 'which, empty, with green turf, and the twin altars she had consecrated'; 'quem' is the relative, the object of 'sacraverat' (pluperfect) with 'geminas… aras' ('twin altars') as a second object; 'inanem' means 'empty': it is a cenotaph, since Hector's body lay in Troy, so the tomb holds no remains. 'causam lacrimis' — 'a cause for tears', an apposition to the whole thing she had set up, with 'lacrimis' in the dative."),

]
